-- Q1: easy / L2 — Self-rental recharacterization rule
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Stern Enterprises LLC is wholly owned by Patricia Stern, who also owns a rental building that she leases to Stern Enterprises for $5,000 per month. The rental activity generates $60,000 of net income during 2026. Under Section 469, how is the $60,000 of rental income recharacterized?', '["Passive income that can offset other passive losses from unrelated activities", "Portfolio income reported on Schedule B of Patricia''s individual return", "Nonpassive income that cannot be used to offset passive losses", "Tax-exempt income excluded from gross income under Section 469"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Treas. Reg. 1.469-2(f)(6), rental income from a self-rental arrangement—where a taxpayer rents property to a business in which the taxpayer materially participates—is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This prevents taxpayers from creating artificial passive income to absorb passive losses from other activities. Choice A is incorrect because the self-rental rule specifically prevents this income from being treated as passive, even though rental activities are generally passive under Section 469.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Evaluate PTP disposition to release suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Novak invested $125,000 in a publicly traded partnership (PTP) five years ago. The PTP has generated cumulative suspended passive losses of $78,000. In 2026, Novak has $12,000 of passive income from the same PTP and is considering selling his entire interest for $140,000. His adjusted basis is $47,000. Evaluate the most advantageous tax treatment upon full disposition.', '["Recognize $93,000 gain and carry the $78,000 suspended losses forward because PTP losses never offset disposition gains", "Release all $78,000 of suspended losses against the $93,000 gain and $12,000 PTP income, resulting in $27,000 net taxable income", "Offset only the $12,000 current-year PTP income, then forfeit the remaining $66,000 permanently upon disposition", "Deduct the $78,000 losses only against the $12,000 PTP income, carrying the remaining $66,000 forward to offset future passive income from any source"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(g), a fully taxable disposition of an entire interest in a passive activity—including a PTP—releases all suspended passive losses. The $78,000 offsets the $93,000 gain and $12,000 PTP income, yielding $27,000 in net taxable income. Any excess suspended losses would be deductible against other income. Choice A is incorrect because Section 469(g) specifically permits suspended losses to offset gain upon a complete disposition, overriding the normal PTP isolation rule of Section 469(k).', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Former passive activity loss offset rule
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Jensen operated a landscaping business through an LLC that was previously passive due to lack of material participation. Jensen had $42,000 of suspended passive losses. In 2026, Jensen begins materially participating and the activity generates $65,000 of net income. How are the $42,000 of formerly suspended losses treated?', '["They offset the $65,000 of active income and any remaining nonpassive income, resulting in $23,000 taxable", "They are permanently disallowed because the activity is no longer classified as passive", "They offset passive income from other activities but not active income from this specific activity", "They offset income from this activity only, resulting in $23,000 taxable from the landscaping business"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(f), when a formerly passive activity becomes nonpassive due to material participation, prior suspended passive losses are allowed only against income from that same activity. The $42,000 offsets the $65,000 income, leaving $23,000 taxable. The losses cannot offset other nonpassive income. Choice A is incorrect because the former passive activity rule limits these losses to income from the specific activity rather than allowing them against all active income generally.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Active participation vs material participation distinction
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469, what distinguishes active participation from material participation?', '["Active participation requires a lower level of involvement, such as approving tenants and lease terms, while material participation requires regular, continuous, and substantial involvement", "Active participation and material participation are identical standards applied to different activity types", "Active participation requires more than 500 hours of involvement annually, while material participation requires only 100 hours", "Active participation applies exclusively to business activities, while material participation applies only to rental activities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(i), active participation is a less stringent standard than material participation, requiring only management decisions such as approving tenants, setting rental terms, or authorizing repairs. Material participation under Treas. Reg. 1.469-5T requires regular, continuous, and substantial involvement, typically measured by specific hourly tests. Choice B is incorrect because active participation is explicitly a lower threshold created to enable the $25,000 rental loss allowance for qualifying taxpayers.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Net investment income tax interaction with passive income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Dr. Evelyn Whitmore has $310,000 of W-2 income, $48,000 of passive rental income, and $22,000 of passive losses from a limited partnership. She files jointly with MAGI of $358,000. What is the net investment income tax under Section 1411 attributable to her passive activities?', '["$0 because rental income is excluded from net investment income", "$4,104 because the 3.8% tax applies to the full $108,000 excess over the $250,000 threshold", "$988 because the 3.8% tax applies to $26,000 of net passive income", "$1,824 because the 3.8% tax applies to $48,000 of gross passive income before netting losses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1411, the 3.8% net investment income tax applies to the lesser of net investment income or the excess of MAGI over the applicable threshold ($250,000 for MFJ). Dr. Whitmore''s net passive income is $26,000 ($48,000 minus $22,000), and her MAGI exceeds the threshold by $108,000. The NIIT is 3.8% multiplied by $26,000, equaling $988. Choice D is incorrect because passive losses properly offset passive income before computing net investment income under Section 1411.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Definition of rental activity under Section 469
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469, which statement correctly defines when an activity is treated as a rental activity?', '["Any activity in which property is rented to a related party under Section 267", "An activity in which customers pay for the use of tangible property and the average period of customer use exceeds seven days", "An activity that generates only rental income regardless of the type of property or services involved", "Any real estate activity, including development and construction projects"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(j)(8) and Treas. Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3), a rental activity generally involves customers paying for the use of tangible property where the average period of customer use exceeds seven days. If the average use period is seven days or less, the activity is treated as a trade or business rather than a rental. Choice D is incorrect because real estate development and construction are trade or business activities, not rental activities, even though they involve real property.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Analyze effect of participation hours when no one else participates more
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Brennan Advisory Group LLC generated a $55,000 net loss during 2026. Owner Callum Brennan participated for 400 hours through June 30 but ceased all involvement on July 1 due to health reasons. His total participation for the year was 400 hours. No other individual participated for more than 350 hours during 2026. Analyze how the $55,000 loss is most likely characterized under Section 469.', '["Nonpassive loss because Brennan participated more hours than any other individual during the tax year", "Passive loss that must be split: $27,500 nonpassive for January through June and $27,500 passive for July through December", "Passive loss because Brennan did not participate for 500 or more hours during 2026", "Nonpassive only if Brennan''s participation exceeded 100 hours and constituted substantially all participation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-5T(a)(3), a taxpayer materially participates if no other individual participated more hours than the taxpayer during the year. Since Brennan''s 400 hours exceeded every other individual''s participation, he satisfies this test regardless of when during the year the hours occurred. The entire $55,000 loss is nonpassive. Choice C is incorrect because the 500-hour test is only one of seven material participation tests; satisfying any single test is sufficient.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — PTP passive loss isolation rule
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Ramos holds interests in two passive activities: a publicly traded partnership (PTP) generating $18,000 in passive losses and a private limited partnership generating $25,000 in passive income. What is the correct treatment of the $18,000 PTP loss under Section 469?', '["The $18,000 PTP loss offsets the $25,000 LP income, leaving $7,000 of net passive income", "The $18,000 PTP loss offsets $18,000 of the LP income with $7,000 remaining taxable", "The $18,000 PTP loss is deductible against Ramos''s active income up to $25,000", "The $18,000 PTP loss is suspended and can only offset future income from the same PTP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(k), passive losses from a publicly traded partnership cannot offset passive income from other sources. PTP losses are ring-fenced and may only be carried forward to offset future passive income from that same PTP. Choice A is incorrect because the netting of passive losses across different activities does not apply when one activity is a PTP; Congress enacted this isolation rule to prevent PTP losses from sheltering other passive income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Working interest in oil and gas exception
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Fletcher Resources LLC holds a working interest in an oil and gas well through a general partnership. Member Quinn Fletcher does not materially participate in the activity. During 2026, the well generates a $35,000 loss. How is this loss treated under Section 469?', '["Passive because Fletcher does not materially participate in the oil and gas activity", "Nonpassive because working interests in oil and gas held without liability limitation are exempt from passive classification", "Deductible only up to $25,000 under the rental real estate allowance", "Passive but may offset portfolio income from dividends and interest"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(c)(3), a working interest in oil and gas property held through a form that does not limit the taxpayer''s liability (such as a general partnership) is not treated as a passive activity regardless of the taxpayer''s participation level. The $35,000 loss is nonpassive and fully deductible subject to at-risk limitations. Choice A is incorrect because the working interest exception specifically exempts this type of activity from passive classification even without material participation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — Passive activity credit limitation with no passive income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Okafor invested $90,000 in a solar energy limited partnership and received a $15,000 passive activity tax credit allocation for 2026. Okafor has no passive income for the year and his regular tax liability attributable to passive activities is $0. How much of the $15,000 credit can Okafor use in 2026?', '["$15,000 because energy credits are exempt from passive activity limitations", "$7,500 because only 50% of passive credits are usable in any single tax year", "$0 because passive credits can only offset tax liability attributable to passive income", "$15,000 because tax credits are treated differently than passive losses under Section 469"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(d)(2), passive activity credits can only offset tax liability attributable to passive income. Since Okafor has no passive income, his regular tax from passive activities is $0, and none of the $15,000 credit is usable in 2026. The unused credit carries forward to future years. Choice A is incorrect because energy credits allocated from a passive activity remain subject to the passive activity credit limitation rules even though certain energy credits have separate provisions under other Code sections.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Personal service corporation passive loss treatment
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under Section 469, how are passive activity losses treated for a personal service corporation?', '["Passive losses may offset only passive income and cannot offset the corporation''s active service income", "Passive losses may offset active service income up to $25,000 annually", "Passive losses are disallowed entirely and may not be carried forward", "Passive losses are passed through to individual shareholders for deduction on their returns"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(a)(2), a personal service corporation (PSC) is subject to passive activity loss rules and may offset passive losses only against passive income. Unlike closely held C corporations, PSCs cannot use passive losses against active business income. Choice B is incorrect because the $25,000 special allowance applies only to individual taxpayers who actively participate in rental real estate activities, not to personal service corporations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Death of PTP holder: suspended losses reduced by basis step-up
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Rivera held an interest in a publicly traded partnership with $52,000 of suspended passive losses accumulated over several years. Rivera dies in 2026, and the PTP interest receives a stepped-up basis increase of $35,000 at death. How much of the suspended passive losses is deductible on Rivera''s final return?', '["$52,000 because all suspended losses are released upon the taxpayer''s death", "$35,000 because only the amount equal to the basis step-up is deductible on the final return", "$0 because PTP losses are permanently disallowed upon death of the interest holder", "$17,000 because suspended losses are reduced by the amount of the basis step-up at death"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(g)(2), when a taxpayer dies, suspended passive losses are allowed on the final return only to the extent they exceed the step-up in basis. Rivera''s $52,000 suspended losses are reduced by the $35,000 basis step-up, leaving $17,000 deductible on the final return. This rule applies equally to PTP interests. Choice A is incorrect because the basis step-up effectively absorbs a portion of the unrealized loss, and allowing the full amount would create a double tax benefit.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — At-risk recapture when distribution reduces amount below zero
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hoffman contributed $80,000 to a partnership and deducted $60,000 of losses in prior years, reducing his at-risk amount to $20,000. In 2026, Hoffman receives a $35,000 cash distribution from the partnership. What at-risk recapture amount must Hoffman include in income for 2026?', '["$15,000 because the distribution reduces the at-risk amount below zero, triggering recapture of previously allowed losses", "$35,000 because the entire distribution is treated as recapture income under Section 465", "$0 because distributions do not affect the at-risk calculation under Section 465(e)", "$20,000 because the entire remaining at-risk amount is recaptured upon any distribution"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 465(e), if a taxpayer''s at-risk amount is reduced below zero—such as by a distribution—the taxpayer must recapture previously deducted losses to the extent the at-risk amount becomes negative. Hoffman''s at-risk amount drops from $20,000 to negative $15,000, so $15,000 of previously allowed losses is recaptured as income. Choice B is incorrect because only the portion that drives the at-risk amount below zero triggers recapture, not the entire distribution amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Which taxpayers are subject to passive activity rules
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469, which of the following taxpayers is subject to the passive activity loss limitation rules?', '["A publicly traded C corporation with thousands of shareholders", "An individual taxpayer with trade or business income", "A tax-exempt organization on its unrelated business income", "A qualified subchapter S subsidiary filing with its parent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(a)(2), the passive activity loss rules apply to individuals, estates, trusts, closely held C corporations, and personal service corporations. Regular C corporations that are not closely held or PSCs are not subject to these limitations. Choice A is incorrect because only closely held C corporations and personal service corporations among C corporations are subject to passive activity rules; widely held C corporations are exempt.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Working interest held through LLC loses the exception
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Garrett Energy Partners LLC, organized as a state-law limited liability company, holds a working interest in an oil well. Member Dalton Reed does not materially participate in the activity. In 2026, the well produces a $42,000 loss. How is this loss treated for Dalton under Section 469?', '["Nonpassive because the working interest exception always applies to oil and gas regardless of entity form", "Nonpassive only if Dalton materially participates in the oil and gas activity", "Passive because the LLC form limits Dalton''s liability, disqualifying the working interest exception under Section 469(c)(3)(B)", "Portfolio loss because oil and gas income is classified separately from passive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(c)(3)(B), the working interest exception for oil and gas applies only if the interest is not held through an entity that limits the taxpayer''s liability. Because Garrett Energy Partners is organized as an LLC, which limits member liability, Dalton cannot use the working interest exception. The $42,000 loss is passive unless Dalton materially participates. Choice A is incorrect because the exception has an explicit liability-limitation exclusion that bars LLC members from automatic nonpassive treatment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Evaluate grouping election to convert passive leasing losses to nonpassive
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Chen operates two activities: a consulting firm in which she materially participates (1,800 hours) and an equipment leasing business (450 hours of participation). The consulting firm generates $280,000 of income, and the leasing business generates $95,000 in losses. Chen''s tax advisor suggests grouping the activities as a single economic unit under Treas. Reg. 1.469-4. Evaluate the primary benefit and risk of making this election.', '["The election converts consulting income to passive, allowing future passive loss offsets from other investments", "The election has no benefit because business activities can always offset each other regardless of participation levels", "The election allows current-year loss deduction but requires recapture of all grouped losses upon disposition of either activity", "The election allows $95,000 of leasing losses to offset consulting income because the combined activity satisfies material participation, but the election is generally irrevocable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-4, taxpayers may group activities into a single economic unit based on factors like common control and business purpose. By grouping, Chen''s combined participation satisfies material participation for the single activity, converting the leasing losses from passive to nonpassive. However, the election is generally irrevocable absent a material change in facts. Choice B is incorrect because activities with and without material participation do not automatically net; passive activity rules require separate analysis absent a grouping election.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluate selective disposition to maximize current-year loss deduction
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Prescott owns three passive activities. Activity A has $80,000 of suspended losses and a $30,000 gain if sold. Activity B has $20,000 of suspended losses and a $60,000 gain if sold. Activity C has $15,000 of suspended losses and a $5,000 loss if sold. Prescott wants to maximize current-year loss recognition against nonpassive income. Evaluate which single disposition is most advantageous.', '["Dispose of Activity A because it releases $80,000 of suspended losses against only $30,000 gain, producing $50,000 deductible against other income", "Dispose of Activity C because the $15,000 suspended losses plus $5,000 current loss yields $20,000 deductible with no offsetting gain", "Dispose of Activity B because the $60,000 gain offsets all suspended losses most efficiently", "Dispose of all three simultaneously to release $115,000 in total suspended losses across activities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(g), a fully taxable disposition releases all suspended passive losses. Disposing Activity A produces the largest net deductible loss: $80,000 of suspended losses less the $30,000 gain equals $50,000 deductible against active and portfolio income. Choice D is incorrect because simultaneously disposing Activity B would generate $40,000 of gain that reduces the net benefit to $30,000. Strategic selective disposition maximizes current-year deductions by avoiding activities with gains exceeding their suspended losses.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Qualified nonrecourse financing definition
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 465(b)(6), what is qualified nonrecourse financing for purposes of the at-risk rules?', '["Any nonrecourse debt secured by personal property used in a trade or business", "Nonrecourse debt backed by a federal government agency regardless of collateral type", "Recourse debt obtained from a related party lender", "Nonrecourse financing from a qualified lender that is secured by real property used in the activity of holding real property"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing is nonrecourse debt from a qualified lender (such as a bank or government agency) that is secured by real property used in the activity of holding real property. This exception allows real estate investors to include certain nonrecourse debt in their at-risk amounts. Choice A is incorrect because the qualified nonrecourse financing exception applies only to real property, not personal property, reflecting Congress''s intent to accommodate standard real estate lending practices.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Analyze effect of becoming RE professional on prior suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Donovan, a former full-time accountant, accumulated $125,000 in suspended passive rental losses over several years. In 2026, Donovan retires from accounting and begins managing rental properties full-time, qualifying as a real estate professional under Section 469(c)(7) and materially participating. His rental properties generate $95,000 of income in 2026. Analyze how the change in status affects the prior suspended losses.', '["All $125,000 of suspended losses are immediately deductible against any income because real estate professional status retroactively reclassifies them", "The $125,000 of suspended losses may offset only the $95,000 of rental income from the now-nonpassive activity, with $30,000 remaining suspended under the former passive activity rule", "The suspended losses are permanently disallowed because they were generated in years when Donovan was not a real estate professional", "The full $125,000 is deductible against all income because qualifying as a real estate professional eliminates all passive activity restrictions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(f), when a formerly passive activity becomes nonpassive because the taxpayer now qualifies as a real estate professional and materially participates, suspended losses from prior years may offset only income from that same activity. The $125,000 offsets $95,000 of rental income, with $30,000 remaining suspended until the activity generates sufficient income. Choice A is incorrect because real estate professional status is not retroactive; it does not reclassify the character of losses from prior passive years.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Compare recourse vs nonrecourse debt at-risk treatment in non-real-estate
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Palmer invested $40,000 cash in a film production partnership and signed a $100,000 personal recourse note to a bank. A second partner, Reeves, invested $40,000 cash but the partnership obtained a $100,000 nonrecourse loan from the same bank for Reeves''s share. Neither partner is in real estate. Analyze the difference in their at-risk amounts.', '["Both have at-risk amounts of $140,000 because bank financing is always included under Section 465", "Both have at-risk amounts of $40,000 because all debt is excluded for non-real-estate activities", "Palmer''s at-risk amount is $140,000 and Reeves''s at-risk amount is $40,000", "Palmer''s at-risk amount is $40,000 and Reeves''s at-risk amount is $140,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 465(b), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount includes cash contributed plus amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer is personally liable (recourse debt). Nonrecourse debt is generally excluded unless it qualifies under the real property exception of Section 465(b)(6). Since film production is not real estate, Reeves''s nonrecourse loan is excluded. Palmer has $140,000 at risk; Reeves has $40,000. Choice A is incorrect because nonrecourse financing increases at-risk amounts only for qualifying real property activities.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Compute at-risk amount after distribution
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Avery contributed $60,000 cash to a partnership and was allocated $15,000 of partnership income in Year 1, increasing her at-risk amount to $75,000. In Year 2, Avery receives a $25,000 cash distribution from the partnership. What is Avery''s at-risk amount after the distribution?', '["$75,000 because distributions do not reduce the at-risk amount under Section 465", "$50,000 because the cash distribution reduces the at-risk amount", "$60,000 because the at-risk amount reverts to the original cash contribution", "$35,000 because both the allocated income and the distribution are subtracted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 465(b), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount is increased by contributions and allocable income, and decreased by distributions and allocable losses. Avery''s at-risk amount of $75,000 is reduced by the $25,000 distribution to $50,000. Choice D is incorrect because previously allocated income increases the at-risk amount permanently and is not subtracted when a distribution occurs; only the distribution itself reduces the amount.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether B&B with extraordinary services escapes rental classification
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Lakewood Hospitality LLC operates a bed-and-breakfast inn where owner Danielle Marsh provides daily housekeeping, prepared meals, guided tours, and concierge services. The average guest stay is four nights. Marsh spent 1,400 hours during 2026 operating the inn, which generated a $68,000 loss. Her AGI is $195,000. Evaluate the most favorable tax classification for this activity.', '["Nonpassive trade or business because extraordinary personal services reclassify the activity as non-rental, and Marsh materially participates with 1,400 hours", "Passive rental subject to the $25,000 special allowance, which is fully phased out at her AGI level", "Passive trade or business because Marsh''s 1,400 hours are insufficient for material participation in a hospitality activity", "Rental activity that qualifies for the real estate professional exception regardless of AGI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(C), a rental activity is reclassified as a non-rental trade or business when extraordinary personal services are provided (meals, housekeeping, tours). Since the activity is non-rental, it is not per se passive. With 1,400 hours, Marsh materially participates under the 500-hour test, making the $68,000 loss nonpassive and fully deductible. Choice B is incorrect because the extraordinary personal services exception removes the activity from rental classification entirely, avoiding the $25,000 allowance limitation and its AGI phase-out.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: hard / L3 — Analyze ordering of basis, at-risk, and passive activity limits
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kenji Yamamoto is an S corporation shareholder with stock basis of $30,000 and a $10,000 loan he made directly to the corporation. The S corporation allocates Kenji a $55,000 loss from a passive activity. Kenji has $12,000 of passive income from another partnership. Analyze the amount deductible on Kenji''s 2026 return and the limiting factor.', '["$55,000 is fully deductible because S corporation losses are not subject to basis limitations", "$12,000 is deductible because the passive activity limitation is the first limit applied, restricting the loss before basis limits", "$40,000 passes basis and at-risk limits, but only $12,000 is deductible after passive limits; $28,000 is suspended under Section 469 and $15,000 is suspended at the basis level", "$40,000 is deductible because basis and at-risk limits restrict the loss to $40,000, and passive rules do not apply to S corporations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the three-tier limitation ordering, S corporation losses are first limited by shareholder basis under Section 1366(d) ($30,000 stock plus $10,000 debt equals $40,000), then by the at-risk rules of Section 465 ($40,000), and finally by the passive activity rules of Section 469. Of the $40,000 passing the first two limits, only $12,000 offsets Kenji''s passive income; the remaining $28,000 is suspended under Section 469 and $15,000 is separately suspended at the basis level. Choice B is incorrect because basis limitations are always applied first, not passive limits.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Analyze grouping election effect on RE professional material participation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Morrison, a real estate professional, owns two rental properties. Property X requires 250 hours of her time and has an on-site manager contributing 400 hours. Property Y requires 350 hours of her time and has an on-site manager contributing 500 hours. She has not made a grouping election. Analyze how making a grouping election would change Morrison''s material participation status.', '["Without the election, Morrison materially participates in Property Y because her 350 hours exceeds the 100-hour minimum threshold", "The grouping election has no effect because real estate professionals are automatically treated as materially participating in all rental activities", "Without the election, Morrison already materially participates in both properties because any participation by a real estate professional counts", "Without the election, neither property meets the 500-hour test and each manager participates more hours; with the election, her combined 600 hours satisfies material participation for the grouped activity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(c)(7), a qualifying real estate professional may treat rental activities as nonpassive only if she materially participates in each rental. Without a grouping election, Morrison''s 250 and 350 individual hours fail both the 500-hour test and the more-than-any-other-individual test for each property. By grouping under Treas. Reg. 1.469-9(g), her combined 600 hours exceeds 500 and exceeds each manager''s individual hours, satisfying material participation. Choice B is incorrect because real estate professional status alone does not satisfy material participation; a separate analysis is required.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Analyze worthlessness as complete disposition releasing suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kessler invested $75,000 in a limited partnership that developed a commercial property. The property''s value has declined to zero and the partnership is being dissolved with no distributions expected. Kessler''s adjusted basis is $75,000 and he has $45,000 of accumulated suspended passive losses. Analyze the tax consequences if the interest becomes completely worthless in 2026.', '["The $75,000 basis loss and $45,000 suspended losses are all released under Section 469(g), totaling $120,000 deductible against any income", "Only the $45,000 of suspended losses are released; the $75,000 basis loss is separately subject to capital loss limitations of $3,000 annually", "The worthlessness triggers only a capital loss; the $45,000 remains suspended until Kessler generates passive income from another source", "Both amounts remain permanently suspended because worthlessness does not qualify as a fully taxable disposition under Section 469(g)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(g)(1), a complete disposition in a fully taxable transaction releases all suspended passive losses. Worthlessness of a partnership interest constitutes a deemed disposition under Section 165, triggering Section 469(g). The $45,000 of suspended losses and the $75,000 basis loss are released and deductible, netting first against passive income, then against nonpassive income. Choice D is incorrect because a worthlessness event is treated as a fully taxable disposition for passive activity purposes, even though no actual sale occurs.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Entities exempt from at-risk rules
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under Section 465, which type of entity is generally exempt from the at-risk limitation rules?', '["S corporations with individual shareholders", "Partnerships composed entirely of individual partners", "Widely held C corporations that are not closely held or personal service corporations", "Single-member LLCs disregarded for federal tax purposes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 465(a), the at-risk rules apply to individuals and certain closely held corporations (including S corporations through their shareholders and personal service corporations). Widely held C corporations that are neither closely held nor personal service corporations are generally exempt from the at-risk limitations. Choice A is incorrect because S corporation losses flow through to individual shareholders, who are themselves subject to at-risk rules at the shareholder level under Section 465.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Analyze related-party sale and suspended loss nonrelease
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Weaver owns a passive rental activity with $55,000 of suspended passive losses and a basis of $120,000. She sells the entire property to her adult son Ethan for $180,000 (FMV), recognizing a $60,000 gain. Analyze whether the disposition releases the suspended passive losses.', '["The $55,000 of suspended losses are released and offset the $60,000 gain, with only $5,000 remaining taxable", "The suspended losses are not released because Section 469(g)(1)(B) excludes dispositions to related parties; the $55,000 remains suspended and the $60,000 gain is passive income", "The $55,000 is permanently disallowed because related-party sales invalidate all prior suspended passive losses", "The $55,000 is released but only to the extent of the $60,000 gain, with $5,000 of the gain converted to ordinary income"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469(g)(1)(B), suspended passive losses are not released when the disposition is to a related party as defined in Section 267(b), which includes a taxpayer''s child. The $55,000 remains suspended, and the $60,000 gain is treated as passive income that can absorb passive losses from this or other activities. Choice A is incorrect because the related-party exception specifically prevents the loss-release mechanism that would otherwise apply in a fully taxable disposition to an unrelated buyer.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Significant participation income recharacterization rule
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Harris participates in three significant participation activities: Activity D (200 hours, $18,000 income), Activity E (180 hours, $12,000 income), and Activity F (160 hours, $8,000 income). Harris''s combined SPA hours total 540. Analyze how the income from these activities is recharacterized under Treas. Reg. 1.469-2T(f)(2).', '["All three activities become nonpassive trade or business activities because total hours exceed 500", "Only Activity D''s income is recharacterized as nonpassive because it has the highest individual participation hours", "The income remains passive because significant participation activities are always passive regardless of aggregate hours", "The net income from the SPAs is recharacterized as nonpassive income, preventing it from being used to offset passive losses from other activities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-2T(f)(2), when a taxpayer''s aggregate participation in significant participation activities exceeds 500 hours, net income from those activities is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This prevents taxpayers from generating artificial passive income through SPAs to absorb passive losses. The $38,000 combined income becomes nonpassive. Choice C is incorrect because the recharacterization rule specifically converts SPA income from passive to nonpassive when the 500-hour aggregate threshold is met, even though the activities remain passive for loss purposes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Evaluate optimal timing for disposition based on marginal rate
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Blackwood has $200,000 of W-2 income, $85,000 of suspended passive losses from Activity G, and $30,000 of passive income from Activity H. In December 2026, Blackwood considers selling Activity G (expected $20,000 gain). In 2027, Blackwood will retire with only $40,000 of pension income. Activity H will generate $30,000 passive income annually. Evaluate the optimal year for disposition.', '["Defer to 2027 because the lower retirement income year maximizes the suspended losses'' value at a lower tax bracket", "Timing is irrelevant because the same $85,000 of suspended losses will be released whenever the disposition occurs", "Sell in 2026 to release all $85,000 while W-2 income is high, because deferral would reduce remaining suspended losses to $55,000 through annual passive netting with Activity H", "Sell in 2026 but elect to carry the freed losses back to 2025 for a refund under passive activity carryback rules"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(g), disposing Activity G in 2026 releases the full $85,000 of suspended losses while Blackwood has $200,000 of W-2 income at a high marginal rate. If deferred to 2027, $30,000 of suspended losses would offset Activity H''s passive income in 2026, reducing the remaining balance to $55,000. In 2027, only $5,000 of net losses would offset pension income at lower rates. Choice A is incorrect because waiting reduces the total suspended losses available for release, and a lower-bracket year provides less tax benefit per dollar of deduction.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Analyze partial disposition of grouped activity
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Patel owns three retail businesses grouped as a single activity under Treas. Reg. 1.469-4. The grouped activity has $40,000 of accumulated suspended passive losses. Patel sells one of the three businesses at a $25,000 gain. Analyze whether the disposition releases any suspended passive losses.', '["The $25,000 gain is passive income from the grouped activity, offsetting $25,000 of the $40,000 suspended losses through normal passive netting; the remaining $15,000 stays suspended", "All $40,000 of suspended losses are released because the sale triggers Section 469(g) for the entire grouped activity", "No suspended losses are released and no gain is recognized because intra-group sales are disregarded under grouping rules", "The $40,000 is released in full and Patel must recapture the losses if the remaining businesses generate income in future years"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Treas. Reg. 1.469-4, grouped activities are treated as a single activity. Selling one component is not a disposition of the entire interest, so Section 469(g) does not apply and suspended losses are not fully released. However, the $25,000 gain is passive income that offsets $25,000 of the $40,000 suspended losses through normal netting. Choice B is incorrect because Section 469(g) requires disposition of the taxpayer''s entire interest in the activity, and the grouped activity continues through the remaining businesses.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
